1) Building:

        Lapack/Blas:

                sudo apt-get install libblas-dev liblapack-dev

        GMP:

                sudo apt-get install libgmp3-dev   

2) Make sure your .cabal test-suite stanza declares it as a build tool dependency: 

        test-suite my-tests
          type:                exitcode-stdio-1.0
          hs-source-dirs:      test
          main-is:             Spec.hs
          build-depends:       base, hspec, your-package-name
          build-tool-depends:  hspec-discover:hspec-discover   

3) Run cabal update so the solver can find and build hspec-discover:

        cabal update

4) Rebuild:

        cabal build all

        Cabal will now build hspec-discover as a dependency and place it on the PATH 
        for the test-suite build. 

        The key piece is build-tool-depends — without it, Cabal v2 doesn't know 
        it needs to make the binary available to GHC.

5) ghci:
        
        5.1) Disable wrong ghci warning about missing modules when loading a .hs file:
                
                (That's the key detail. The warning is a known false positive in the REPL context (tracked as Cabal issue #4711))

                :set -Wno-missing-home-modules   

        5.2) cd into the same directory where stearnswharf.cabal resides

        5.3) cabal ghci

        5.4) :l src/StearnsWharf.Beam


6) Better records:

        6.1) In ghci:

                :set -XNoFieldSelectors -XOverloadedRecordDot 

        6.2) In .hs files:

                {-# LANGUAGE DuplicateRecordFields #-}
                {-# LANGUAGE OverloadedRecordDot #-}
                {-# LANGUAGE OverloadedRecordUpdate #-}
                {-# LANGUAGE NoFieldSelectors #-}
                
        6.3) To access records, use:

                data User  = User  { name :: Text, uid :: Int } deriving Generic

                alice = User { name = "Alice", uid = 3 }

                putStrLn alice.name
                
        or 

        6.3) To access records, use lenses:

                data User  = User  { name :: Text, uid :: Int } deriving Generic

                data Group = Group { name :: Text, gid :: Int } deriving Generic

                -- Access via overloaded labels — no ambiguity, no TH: 

                view #name (user  :: User)   -- "Alice"
                view #name (group :: Group)  -- "eng"

                set #uid 42 user

                
        
## Beregning – IPE-profil, 2 m spennvidde, 9,75 kN/m

**Antakelser:** Fritt oppsatt bære (enkeltspenn), S235 (fy = 235 MPa), γM₀ = 1,0.

### 1. Bøyemoment

$$M_{Ed} = \frac{wL^2}{8} = \frac{9{,}75 \times 2^2}{8} = 4{,}875 \text{ kN·m}$$

Krevd plastisk tverrsnittsmodul:

$$W_{pl,req} = \frac{M_{Ed}}{f_y} = \frac{4{,}875 \times 10^6}{235} = 20{,}7 \text{ cm}^3$$

Allerede **IPE 80** (W_pl = 24,5 cm³) er tilstrekkelig for bøyning.

### 2. Defleksjon (styrer dimensjoneringen!)

Tillatt defleksjon: L/250 = 2000/250 = **8 mm**

Krevd tverrsnittsmoment:

$$I_{req} = \frac{5wL^4}{384E \cdot \delta_{max}} = \frac{5 \times 9{,}75 \times 2000^4}{384 \times 210\,000 \times 8} \approx 1\,210 \text{ cm}^4$$

| Profil | I_y (cm⁴) | δ_max (mm) | L/δ | OK? |
|--------|-----------|------------|-----|-----|
| IPE 160 | 869 | 11,1 | L/180 | ✗ |
| **IPE 180** | **1 317** | **7,3** | **L/274** | **✓** |
| IPE 200 | 1 943 | 5,0 | L/404 | ✓ |

### Konklusjon

**IPE 180** er den minste IPE-profilen som oppfyller både bøyning og defleksjon (L/250).

> ⚠️ Selvvekten (18,8 kg/m ≈ 0,19 kN/m) er forsvinnende liten mot 9,75 kN/m og kan ignoreres. Hvis defleksjonskravet er L/300 i stedet, holder IPE 160.



